152	\vspace{6pt}
153	
154	% =====================================================================
155	\section{4.1\quad Language Overview}
156	
157	\textbf{Name.}\, ANS --- short for \textit{Atomic Notation Script}.
158	
159	\textbf{Domain.}\, ANS is a small DSL for declaring chemical elements,
160	compounds, and reactions, verifying balance at compile time, and performing
161	stoichiometric queries such as molar mass and theoretical yield. It targets
162	undergraduate chemistry students and instructors who want to write
163	calculations the way they appear in textbooks rather than as bare numerical
164	code.
165	
166	\subsection{Sample program}
167	\begin{lstlisting}
168	# Declare elements
169	element H { atomic_mass = 1.008,  valence = 1, symbol = "H" };
170	element O { atomic_mass = 15.999, valence = 2, symbol = "O" };
171	element C { atomic_mass = 12.011, valence = 4, symbol = "C" };
172	
173	# Declare compounds
174	compound water   { formula = H2O, name = "Water"          };
175	compound methane { formula = CH4, name = "Methane"        };
176	compound co2     { formula = CO2, name = "Carbon dioxide" };
177	compound oxygen  { formula = O2,  name = "Oxygen"         };
178	
179	# Variables and a heterogeneous list
180	let n        = 21.2;
181	let elements = [H, O, C];
182	let list     = [1, "two", 3.45, water, methane.formula, co2.name, H];
183	
184	# A user-defined function
185	function molar_mass(compound) {
186	    let formula = compound.formula;
187	    let mass    = 0.0;
188	    for (let element in formula) {
189	        mass += element.atomic_mass * formula[element];
190	    }
191	    return mass;
192	};
193	
194	# A reaction whose balance is checked at compile time
195	reaction combustion {
196	    reactants = CH4 + 2*O2,
197	    products  = CO2 + 2*H2O,
198	    check     = balanced,
199	    energy    = exothermic 890.3 kJ/mol
200	};
201	
202	# Stoichiometric queries
203	molar_mass(water);
204	compute_yield(combustion, 5.0 mol, CH4);
205	
206	# Conditional reporting
207	if compute_yield(combustion, 5.0 mol, CH4) > 0 {
208	    print "The yield of CH4 is positive, so combustion is possible.";
209	} elif compute_yield(combustion, 5.0 mol, CH4) == 0 {
210	    print "The yield is zero.";
211	} else {
212	    print "The yield is negative.";
213	}
214	\end{lstlisting}
215	
216	\subsection{Sebesta evaluation-criteria justification (Ch.~1)}
217	
218	Following Sebesta's Chapter~1 evaluation framework --- readability,
219	writability, reliability, and overall cost --- ANS deliberately prioritizes
220	\textbf{readability, writability, and reliability} while knowingly sacrificing
221	some \textbf{cost, generality, and orthogonality}. Readability is favoured
222	because the syntax mirrors chemistry vocabulary directly (\code{element},
223	\code{compound}, \code{reaction}, \code{reactants}, \code{products},
224	\code{balanced}) on top of a familiar C-like skeleton (\code{let}, braces,
225	semicolons, function calls, \code{if}/\code{elif}/\code{else}), so the meaning
226	of a program can be inferred from its surface form. Writability is favoured
227	because common domain tasks --- declaring an element, writing a balanced
228	equation as \code{CH4 + 2*O2 -> CO2 + 2*H2O}, or asking
229	\code{molar\_mass(water)} --- are first-class constructs rather than
230	user-coded library routines. Reliability is favoured through compile-time
231	validation: the lexer recognises formula and unit literals as their own
232	categories, the semantic checker verifies that every element symbol exists in
233	the periodic table and that every reaction is atomically balanced before any
234	value is computed, and units such as \code{mol} and \code{kJ/mol} are tracked
235	rather than discarded. The accepted trade-offs are the higher implementation
236	cost of formula parsing, unit handling, and balance checking, the loss of
237	generality (ANS is unsuited to non-chemistry tasks), and reduced orthogonality
238	(many fixed domain forms instead of a small set of freely combinable
239	primitives). Sebesta explicitly notes that language design is a process of
240	selecting trade-offs among these criteria; for a teaching-oriented chemistry
241	DSL the gains in domain-specific clarity and safety outweigh the loss of
242	general-purpose expressiveness.
243	
244	% =====================================================================
245	\section{4.2\quad Lexical Structure}
246	
247	ANS uses the standard five-category lexical split --- identifiers, literals,
248	keywords, operators, and separators --- that Sebesta describes as the output
249	of a finite-automaton-based lexical analyser (\S4.2). Two practical wrinkles
250	drive the design: (i)~chemical formulas like \code{H2O} must be
251	distinguishable from user-defined names, and (ii)~several chemistry words
252	(\code{reaction}, \code{compound}, \code{mol}) must still be usable as
253	ordinary parameter names inside function bodies. ANS solves (i) by case
254	(identifiers begin lowercase; element/formula tokens begin uppercase) and
255	(ii)~by treating the chemistry words as \textit{contextual} keywords that the
256	parser promotes only in the grammar positions where they are reserved.
257	
258	\renewcommand{\tabularxcolumn}[1]{>{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}m{#1}}
259	% A small helper so a regex column entry is set in monospace, scriptsize,
260	% and broken at any character if needed.
261	\newcommand{\rx}[1]{\scriptsize\ttfamily\seqsplit{#1}}
262	{\footnotesize
263	\rowcolors{2}{white}{ansrow}
264	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}
265	\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{2.7cm}
266	                                >{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{6.0cm}
267	                                Y@{}}
268	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}\toprule
269	\rowcolor{ansblue}\thd{Category} & \thd{ANS meaning / examples} & \thd{Pattern (Python-style regex)}\\
270	\midrule
271	Identifiers
272	  & User-defined names: \code{water}, \code{methane}, \code{num}, \code{reactant\_mol}
273	  & \rx{[a-z\_][A-Za-z0-9\_]*}\\
274	Number literals
275	  & Integers, decimals, scientific: \code{20}, \code{1.008}, \code{6.02e23}
276	  & \rx{(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*)(?:\textbackslash.[0-9]+)?(?:[eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?}\\
277	String literals
278	  & \code{"Hydrogen"}, \code{"Water"}, \code{"Carbon dioxide"}
279	  & \rx{"(?:[\^{}"\textbackslash\textbackslash{}\textbackslash{}r\textbackslash{}n]|\textbackslash\textbackslash["\textbackslash\textbackslash{}nrt])*"}\\
280	Char literals
281	  & \code{'H'}, \code{'\textbackslash{}n'}
282	  & \rx{'(?:[\^{}'\textbackslash\textbackslash{}\textbackslash{}r\textbackslash{}n]|\textbackslash\textbackslash['\textbackslash\textbackslash{}nrt])'}\\
283	Formula literals
284	  & Element symbols / formulas: \code{H}, \code{O}, \code{H2O}, \code{CH4}, \code{CO2}, \code{O2}
285	  & \rx{(?:[A-Z][a-z]?(?:[1-9][0-9]*)?)+}\\
286	Unit literals
287	  & \code{mol}, \code{g}, \code{kg}, \code{g/mol}, \code{kg/mol}, \code{kJ}, \code{kJ/mol}
288	  & \rx{(?<![A-Za-z0-9\_])(?:kg/mol|g/mol|kJ/mol|mol|kg|g|kJ)(?![A-Za-z0-9\_])}\\
289	Keywords (hard)
290	  & \code{let}, \code{function}, \code{return}, \code{for}, \code{in}, \code{if}, \code{elif}, \code{else}, \code{print}
291	  & matched as identifier, then keyword-table lookup\\
292	Keywords (contextual)
293	  & \code{element}, \code{compound}, \code{reaction}, \code{reactants},
294	    \code{products}, \code{formula}, \code{name}, \code{atomic\_mass},
295	    \code{valence}, \code{symbol}, \code{check}, \code{balanced},
296	    \code{energy}, \code{exothermic}, \code{endothermic}
297	  & promoted to keywords only in their grammar positions\\
298	Operators
299	  & \code{=}\, \code{+}\, \code{-}\, \code{*}\, \code{/}\, \code{+=}\, \code{-=}\, \code{*=}\, \code{/=}\, \code{==}\, \code{!=}\, \code{<}\, \code{<=}\, \code{>}\, \code{>=}
300	  & \rx{==|!=|<=|>=|\textbackslash{}+=|-=|\textbackslash{}*=|/=|=|[+\textbackslash{}-*/<>]}\\
301	Separators
302	  & \code{\{}\, \code{\}}\, \code{(}\, \code{)}\, \code{[}\, \code{]}\, \code{,}\, \code{;}\, \code{.}
303	  & \rx{[\{\}()\textbackslash{}[\textbackslash{}],;.]}\\
304	Comments
305	  & \code{\#} line comment to end of line
306	  & \rx{\#[\^{}\textbackslash{}r\textbackslash{}n]*}\\
307	Whitespace
308	  & spaces, tabs, newlines (separator only, not a token)
309	  & \rx{[\textbackslash{} \textbackslash{}t\textbackslash{}f\textbackslash{}r\textbackslash{}n]+}\\
310	\bottomrule
311	\end{tabularx}}
312	\par\vspace{4pt}
313	
314	\textbf{Lexer ordering rules.}\, Match the longer pattern first within each
315	group: \code{kJ/mol} before \code{kJ}; \code{g/mol} before \code{g}; compound
316	assignments such as \code{+=} before \code{+}; and \code{==}, \code{<=},
317	\code{>=}, \code{!=} before their single-character forms. Identifiers are
318	matched first, then promoted to \textit{KEYWORD} if their lexeme is in the
319	reserved-word table --- Sebesta's standard technique for names vs. reserved
320	words (\S5.1).
321	
322	% =====================================================================
323	\newpage
324	\section{4.3\quad Syntax (EBNF)}
325	
326	The grammar below is written in Sebesta-style EBNF (\S3.1--3.2): non-terminals
327	appear in angle brackets, terminals in double quotes, \code{\{ X \}} means
328	zero or more repetitions of X, \code{[ X ]} means optional, and \code{|}
329	separates alternatives. The grammar is unambiguous for the implemented
330	subset; ambiguity-resolution decisions are listed at the end of this section.
331	
332	\begin{lstlisting}[style=ebnf]
333	(* ============================================================
334	   ANS Grammar (Part 1) -- Sebesta-style EBNF
335	   ============================================================ *)
336	
337	(* ---------- Program structure ---------- *)
338	<program>           ::= { <top_level_statement> } EOF
339	
340	<top_level_statement> ::= <variable_declaration> ";"
341	                        | <assignment_statement>  ";"
342	                        | <element_declaration>   ";"
343	                        | <compound_declaration>  ";"
344	                        | <reaction_declaration>  ";"
345	                        | <function_declaration>
346	                        | <if_statement>
347	                        | <expression_statement>  ";"
348	
349	(* ---------- Blocks and statements ---------- *)
350	<block>             ::= "{" { <statement> } "}"
351	
352	<statement>         ::= <variable_declaration>           ";"
353	                      | <assignment_statement>           ";"
354	                      | <augmented_assignment_statement> ";"
355	                      | <return_statement>               ";"
356	                      | <for_statement>
357	                      | <if_statement>
358	                      | <print_statement>                ";"
359	                      | <expression_statement>           ";"
360	
361	<variable_declaration>  ::= "let" <identifier> "=" <expression>
362	<assignment_statement>  ::= <assignable> "=" <expression>
363	<augmented_assignment_statement>
364	                        ::= <assignable> <aug_op> <expression>
365	<aug_op>                ::= "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/="
366	<return_statement>      ::= "return" [ <expression> ]
367	<print_statement>       ::= "print" <expression>
368	<expression_statement>  ::= <expression>
369	
370	(* ---------- Functions and loops ---------- *)
371	<function_declaration> ::= "function" <identifier>
372	                           "(" [ <parameter_list> ] ")" <block> ";"
373	<parameter_list>       ::= <identifier> { "," <identifier> }
374	
375	<for_statement>        ::= "for" "(" "let" <identifier> "in" <expression> ")"
376	                           <block>
377	
378	(* ---------- Conditionals (no dangling-else: braces required) ---------- *)
379	<if_statement>         ::= "if" <expression> <block>
380	                           { "elif" <expression> <block> }
381	                           [ "else" <block> ]
382	
383	(* ---------- Domain-specific declarations ---------- *)
384	<element_declaration>  ::= "element" <element_symbol>
385	                           "{" <element_field_list> "}"
386	<element_symbol>       ::= <formula_literal>
387	<element_field_list>   ::= <element_field> { "," <element_field> } [ "," ]
388	<element_field>        ::= "atomic_mass" "=" <number_literal>
389	                         | "valence"     "=" <number_literal>
390	                         | "symbol"      "=" <string_literal>
391	
392	<compound_declaration> ::= "compound" <identifier>
393	                           "{" <compound_field_list> "}"
394	<compound_field_list>  ::= <compound_field> { "," <compound_field> } [ "," ]
395	<compound_field>       ::= "formula" "=" <formula_literal>
396	                         | "name"    "=" <string_literal>
397	
398	<reaction_declaration> ::= "reaction" <identifier>
399	                           "{" <reaction_field_list> "}"
400	<reaction_field_list>  ::= <reaction_field> { "," <reaction_field> } [ "," ]
401	<reaction_field>       ::= "reactants" "=" <reaction_expression>
402	                         | "products"  "=" <reaction_expression>
403	                         | "check"     "=" <balance_check>
404	                         | "energy"    "=" <energy_expression>
405	<balance_check>        ::= "balanced"
406	<energy_expression>    ::= <energy_kind> <quantity_literal>
407	<energy_kind>          ::= "exothermic" | "endothermic"
408	
409	(* ---------- Reaction expressions (own grammar, separate from arithmetic) ---------- *)
410	<reaction_expression>  ::= <reaction_term> { "+" <reaction_term> }
411	<reaction_term>        ::= <reaction_species>
412	                         | <coefficient> "*" <reaction_species>
413	<coefficient>          ::= <number_literal>
414	<reaction_species>     ::= <formula_literal> | <identifier>
415	
416	(* ---------- General expressions (precedence encoded in the grammar) ---------- *)
417	<expression>                ::= <or_expression>
418	<or_expression>             ::= <and_expression>
419	                                { "||" <and_expression> }
420	<and_expression>            ::= <equality_expression>
421	                                { "&&" <equality_expression> }
422	<equality_expression>       ::= <relational_expression>
423	                                { ("==" | "!=") <relational_expression> }
424	<relational_expression>     ::= <additive_expression>
425	                                { ("<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=") <additive_expression> }
426	<additive_expression>       ::= <multiplicative_expression>
427	                                { ("+" | "-") <multiplicative_expression> }
428	<multiplicative_expression> ::= <unary_expression>
429	                                { ("*" | "/") <unary_expression> }
430	<unary_expression>          ::= [ "-" ] <postfix_expression>
431	<postfix_expression>        ::= <primary_expression> { <postfix_op> }
432	<postfix_op>                ::= "." <identifier>
433	                              | "[" <expression> "]"
434	                              | "(" [ <argument_list> ] ")"
435	
436	<primary_expression>        ::= <quantity_literal>
437	                              | <number_literal>
438	                              | <string_literal>
439	                              | <char_literal>
440	                              | <formula_literal>
441	                              | <list_literal>
442	                              | <identifier>
443	                              | "(" <expression> ")"
444	
445	<argument_list>             ::= <expression> { "," <expression> }
446	<quantity_literal>          ::= <number_literal> <unit_literal>
447	<list_literal>              ::= "[" [ <expression> { "," <expression> } ] "]"
448	
449	(* ---------- Assignable LHS (subset of postfix; no calls allowed) ---------- *)
450	<assignable>          ::= <identifier> { <assignable_postfix> }
451	<assignable_postfix>  ::= "." <identifier>
452	                        | "[" <expression> "]"
453	
454	(* ---------- Lexical (terminals from the lexer) ---------- *)
455	<identifier>      ::= IDENTIFIER       (* [a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*           *)
456	<number_literal>  ::= NUMBER_LITERAL
457	<string_literal>  ::= STRING_LITERAL
458	<char_literal>    ::= CHAR_LITERAL
459	<formula_literal> ::= FORMULA_LITERAL  (* (?:[A-Z][a-z]?(?:[1-9][0-9]*)?)+ *)
460	<unit_literal>    ::= UNIT_LITERAL
461	\end{lstlisting}
462	
463	\subsection{Ambiguity-resolution annotations}
464	
465	{\footnotesize
466	\rowcolors{2}{white}{ansrow}
467	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}
468	\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{4.0cm} Y@{}}
469	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}\toprule
470	\rowcolor{ansblue}\thd{Source of ambiguity} & \thd{How ANS resolves it}\\
471	\midrule
472	Operator precedence
473	  & Encoded by the cascade
474	    \textit{or} $\to$ \textit{and} $\to$ equality $\to$ relational $\to$
475	    additive $\to$ multiplicative $\to$ unary $\to$ postfix $\to$ primary.
476	    Therefore \code{a + b * c} parses as \code{a + (b * c)} and
477	    \code{a < b + c} parses as \code{a < (b + c)}. All binary operators are
478	    left-associative because each level repeats with \code{\{ ... \}}.\\
479	Reaction vs. arithmetic +/*
480	  & Reactions have their own non-terminal \code{<reaction\_expression>},
481	    separate from \code{<expression>}. Inside a reaction, \code{+} separates
482	    chemical species and \code{*} binds a coefficient to a formula, so
483	    \code{CH4 + 2*O2} parses unambiguously as \code{CH4 + (2*O2)}.\\
484	Stoichiometric coefficient vs. subscript
485	  & ANS requires the explicit form \code{2*O2} rather than \code{2O2}. This
486	    removes the ambiguity between a coefficient and a leading digit of a
487	    (non-existent) formula token, and keeps \code{FORMULA\_LITERAL} purely
488	    uppercase-led.\\
489	Formula literal vs. identifier
490	  & Identifiers begin with a lowercase letter or underscore; formula literals
491	    begin with an uppercase letter. Thus \code{water} is always an identifier
492	    and \code{H2O} is always a formula. A bare \code{H} is lexically a
493	    \code{FORMULA\_LITERAL}; the parser interprets it as an
494	    \code{<element\_symbol>} only when it follows the keyword \code{element}.\\
495	Contextual keywords
496	  & \code{reaction}, \code{compound}, \code{mol}, \code{formula}, etc.\ are
497	    recognised as keywords only in the grammar positions where they appear
498	    above. Outside those positions they remain identifiers, which is why
499	    \code{function compute\_yield(reaction, mol, compound) \{ ... \}} is
500	    legal.\\
501	Dangling-else
502	  & ANS requires braces around every then/elif/else body
503	    (see \code{<if\_statement>}). The classical dangling-else ambiguity
504	    therefore cannot arise: every \code{else} attaches to a uniquely
505	    delimited \code{<block>}.\\
506	Function call vs. assignment target
507	  & \code{<assignable>} deliberately omits the call postfix
508	    \code{'(' ... ')'}. Therefore \code{f(x) = e} is a syntax error,
509	    removing the ambiguity between calling a function and assigning to its
510	    result.\\
511	Field order in declarations
512	  & \code{<element\_field\_list>}, \code{<compound\_field\_list>}, and
513	    \code{<reaction\_field\_list>} accept fields in any order syntactically;
514	    the semantic checker enforces that each required field appears exactly
515	    once. This keeps the grammar LL(1) while still rejecting duplicate or
516	    missing fields with a precise error message.\\
517	\bottomrule
518	\end{tabularx}}
519	
520	% =====================================================================
521	\newpage
522	\section{4.6\quad Names, Binding, Scope, Lifetime}
523	
524	\subsection{Rules for legal identifiers (Sebesta \S5.1)}
525	
526	An ANS identifier matches \code{[a-z\_][A-Za-z0-9\_]*}: it begins with a
527	lowercase letter or underscore and continues with letters, digits, or
528	underscores. ANS is \textbf{case-sensitive}, so \code{water} and \code{Water}
529	are distinct names. Tokens that begin with an uppercase letter (e.g.\
530	\code{H}, \code{O2}, \code{H2O}) are \textit{formula literals}, never
531	identifiers, which keeps chemistry notation visually separated from program
532	names. The hard reserved words \code{let, function, return, for, in, if,
533	elif, else, print} may never be used as identifiers. The chemistry words
534	\code{element, compound, reaction, reactants, products, formula, name,
535	atomic\_mass, valence, symbol, check, balanced, energy, exothermic,
536	endothermic} are \textit{contextual} keywords in the sense of Sebesta's
537	discussion of keywords vs.\ reserved words: they are reserved only where the
538	grammar expects them, so
539	\code{function compute\_yield(reaction, mol, compound) \{ ... \}} is a legal
540	declaration. A name may not be declared twice in the same scope; a name in
541	an inner scope may shadow an outer name, and the type checker emits a warning
542	when this shadows a top-level chemistry declaration.
543	
544	\subsection{Bindings: compile time vs.\ run time (Sebesta \S5.2)}
545	
546	{\footnotesize
547	\rowcolors{2}{white}{ansrow}
548	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}
549	\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{5.4cm}
550	                               >{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{4.0cm}
551	                               Y@{}}
552	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}\toprule
553	\rowcolor{ansblue}\thd{Binding} & \thd{When} & \thd{Example}\\
554	\midrule
555	Operator symbol $\to$ meaning
556	  & Language-design time
557	  & \code{+}, \code{*}, \code{==}, \code{\&\&}\\
558	Lexeme $\to$ token category
559	  & Lex / compile time
560	  & \code{water} $\to$ IDENTIFIER, \code{H2O} $\to$ FORMULA\_LITERAL\\
561	Identifier $\to$ declaration
562	  & Compile time (static scope walk)
563	  & \code{mass} refers to its enclosing \code{let}\\
564	Variable $\to$ inferred type
565	  & Compile time
566	  & \code{let n = 21.2;} $\Rightarrow$ \code{n : Float}\\
567	Element / compound / reaction name $\to$ declaration
568	  & Compile time
569	  & \code{water}, \code{combustion}\\
570	Formula validity, balance check, unit compatibility
571	  & Compile time
572	  & \code{check = balanced} verified statically\\
573	Parameter $\to$ argument value
574	  & Run time
575	  & \code{compute\_yield(combustion, 5.0 mol, CH4)}\\
576	Local variable $\to$ memory cell
577	  & Run time (on entry)
578	  & stack-allocated when block is entered\\
579	Variable $\to$ value
580	  & Run time
581	  & \code{mass += element.atomic\_mass * formula[element]}\\
582	Function name $\to$ return value
583	  & Run time
584	  & \code{return product\_mol;}\\
585	\bottomrule
586	\end{tabularx}}
587	\par\vspace{2pt}
588	
589	ANS therefore uses mostly \textbf{static} bindings for names, types, and
590	chemistry validation, and \textbf{dynamic} bindings for storage and values
591	--- exactly the mix Sebesta recommends when reliability is more important
592	than late flexibility (\S5.4.3).
593	
594	\subsection{Scoping rule: static (lexical) scoping (Sebesta \S5.5)}
595	
596	ANS uses \textbf{static, lexical scoping}. The reference of every name is
597	determined from the program text alone: a name resolves to the nearest
598	enclosing declaration in the source --- block, loop body, function body,
599	then enclosing function (if nested), then top level. The reasoning follows
600	Sebesta's argument that static scope makes both the human reader and the
601	compiler able to determine the meaning of every name without simulating
602	execution.
603	
604	\textbf{What would break under dynamic scoping.}\, Consider:
605	
606	\begin{lstlisting}
607	let water = methane;          # 'water' shadowed at top level
608	
609	function f() {
610	    return molar_mass(water);  # what does 'water' refer to?
611	}
612	
613	function g() {
614	    let water = co2;
615	    return f();                # caller introduces a local 'water'
616	}
617	\end{lstlisting}
618	
619	Under static scoping \code{f} always uses the top-level \code{water} visible
620	where it was \textit{defined}, so \code{g()} still computes the molar mass of
621	methane. Under dynamic scoping \code{f} would instead capture \code{g}'s
622	local \code{water} and silently switch to CO\textsubscript{2}. For a chemistry
623	DSL this is intolerable: a stoichiometric calculation must not depend on the
624	call chain. Dynamic scoping would also defeat the compile-time
625	formula-validity and balance checks, because the type checker could no longer
626	determine which compound a function will read at run time. ANS therefore
627	commits to static scoping; the small loss of run-time flexibility is exactly
628	the trade-off named in \S4.1.
629	
630	\subsection{Lifetimes (Sebesta \S5.4.3)}
631	
632	{\footnotesize
633	\rowcolors{2}{white}{ansrow}
634	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}
635	\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{5.0cm}
636	                               >{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}p{4.0cm}
637	                               Y@{}}
638	\arrayrulecolor{ansborder}\toprule
639	\rowcolor{ansblue}\thd{Entity} & \thd{Lifetime category} & \thd{Notes}\\
640	\midrule
641	Top-level element / compound / reaction declarations
642	  & Static (program lifetime)
643	  & Allocated once at program start; visible to every later statement and
644	    function.\\
645	Top-level \code{let} variables
646	  & Static (program lifetime)
647	  & Single binding from the \code{let} until program termination.\\
648	Function parameters
649	  & Stack-dynamic
650	  & Allocated on call; deallocated on return. Supports recursion.\\
651	\code{let} variables inside a function or block
652	  & Stack-dynamic
653	  & Allocated when the declaration is elaborated; freed when the enclosing
654	    block exits.\\
655	Loop variable in \code{for (let x in ...)}
656	  & Stack-dynamic, scoped to the loop
657	  & A fresh binding per iteration, so closures over \code{x} do not alias.\\
658	List values, compound objects, reaction objects
659	  & Heap-managed by the implementation
660	  & Not user-visible: ANS has no \code{new} / \code{delete} and no pointers,
661	    so heap management is automatic, not ``explicit heap-dynamic'' in
662	    Sebesta's sense.\\
663	\bottomrule
664	\end{tabularx}}
665	\par\vspace{2pt}
666	
667	Local variables are \textbf{stack-dynamic} rather than static so that
668	repeated calls to \code{molar\_mass} or \code{compute\_yield} cannot leak
669	state from one invocation to the next, and so that recursion (anticipated for
670	Part~2's reaction search and balance solver) is supported. ANS deliberately
671	exposes no \textbf{explicit heap-dynamic} construct: there are no pointers,
672	no \code{new}/\code{delete}, and no aliasing. This costs some flexibility but
673	eliminates an entire class of unreliability errors --- a trade-off that is
674	consistent with the readability/reliability prioritisation declared in
675	\S4.1.
676	
677	\vspace{6pt}
678	{\footnotesize\itshape\color{anscaption}
679	Part~2 will extend this document with \S4.4 Semantics, \S4.5 Type System,
680	\S4.7 Expressions \& Assignment, and \S4.8 Design Rationale, all consistent
681	with the decisions stated above.\par}
682	
683	\end{document}
684

